courses.learning_outcomes:
1) Analyze the logic and assumptions law’s relationship to gender, sexuality, class, and ethnicity;
2) Contextualize the meanings of gender and sexuality embedded in juridical processes within a historical and cross-cultural perspective;
3) Understand law as social processes, legal documents as ethnographic artifacts, and legal procedures as techniques of legitimation;
4) Develop skills to critically examine the making of laws, their ways of enforcement, and their impact on people.

After completing this course students will:

• Understand the origins, development and fundamental ideas underlying the concepts of “landscape”, “cultural landscape” and “social landscape” and how they are related;

• In general terms be able to identify and explain the key differences in Western and non-Western (including Chinese) understandings and valuing of landscapes, and then apply and evaluate them in terms of real-life case studies;

• Recognise that landscape is a powerful concept connecting scholars across multiple disciplines, and an ‘arena’ where conflicting views, values, and meanings are negotiated by a range of different stakeholders, for example government legislators and curators, ‘expert’ consultants, and local community groups;

• Appreciate the importance of the ‘social landscape approach’ as a means of understanding multi-period cultural landscapes in terms of recursive (feedback) interactions between human agency and natural processes;

• Recognise that cultural landscapes materially reflect human choices and value-judgements across space and through time, and can thus reveal what past and present communities, organisations and governments chose to keep and protect and what they let go, and most importantly why;

• More fully understand the value of ‘landscape’, not only in terms of its critical importance to—among others—anthropologists, archaeologists and heritage managers, but also its key role in our day-to-day engagements with and understandings of the world about us as social beings.

