courses.learning_outcomes:
Toward the end of the course, students are expected to be able to:
1) understand the basic concepts of gender and how gender is culturally constructed in particular societies;
2) be aware of how gender and sexuality work as guiding principles in daily lives;
3) apply social theories to explain gender phenomena of particular cultures, particularly of Asian societies.
4) be aware of the impact of globalization on women’s lives and experience;
5) analyze a range of gender issues from a global perspective.

Students taking this course will:
1) learn to see the cultural nature of reality and of the supernatural;
2) understand how many apparently irrational and bizarre beliefs make sense in their cultural contexts;
3) learn how to use critical thinking while at the same time being tolerant of other views;
4) develop a healthy scientific skepticism, while at the same time remaining open to new ideas;
5) be able to analyze other cases of new and unusual beliefs that will emerge later in life.

1) Students will be able to apply the main theoretical concepts of human rights and violence to explain the relevance of human rights in modern international politics;
2) Students will be able to develop solid knowledge of the emergence of new forms of international relations centered on human rights discourses such as the Declaration of Human Rights in 1948, international justice trials, etc;
3) Students will be able to analyse the key debates about the universality and relevance of human rights, and apply them in their research;
4) Students will be able to critically examine political violence and human rights issues in the global and local context.

